+++
title: Alexander the Great
description: King of Macedon from 336 to 323 BC
hook: He was undefeated in battle, and by the age of 30 he had created one of
  the largest empires in history, stretching from Greece to northwestern
  India.
standfirst: "Alexander III of Macedon was king from 336 BC until his death:
  the son of Philip II by his fourth wife, Olympias, on the throne at the age
  of 20 after his father was assassinated, and campaigning through Asia and
  Egypt for most of the reign that followed."
key-facts:
  - name: King of Macedon
    value: October 336 – June 323 BC, following Philip II
  - name: Born
    value: 20 or 21 July 356 BC, Pella, Macedon
  - name: Died
    value: 10 or 11 June 323 BC (aged 32), Babylon, Macedonian Empire
  - name: Parents
    value: Philip II of Macedon and Olympias of Epirus, of the Argead dynasty
  - name: Also ruled as
    value: Pharaoh of Egypt, 332–323 BC, and King of Persia, 330–323 BC
  - name: Married to
    value: Roxana, Stateira and Parysatis
pull-quotes:
  - text: My boy, you must find a kingdom big enough for your ambitions. Macedon
      is too small for you
    attribution: Philip II, on watching the ten-year-old Alexander tame
      Bucephalas, as reported by Plutarch
    section: lineage-and-childhood
  - text: This so irritated Alexander that throwing one of the cups at his head,
      "You villain," said he, "what, am I then a bastard?"
    attribution: Plutarch, describing the feud at Philip's wedding
    section: exile-and-return
  - text: But verily, if I were not Alexander, I would like to be Diogenes.
    attribution: Alexander, on Diogenes the Cynic, who had asked him to stop
      blocking the sunlight
    section: consolidation-of-power
  - text: I shall not enter your houses
    attribution: The message "the king of the world, Alexander" sent ahead to the
      people of Babylon, in the Babylonian astronomical diaries
    section: assyria-and-babylonia
  - text: Shall I pass by and leave you lying there because of the expeditions you
      led against Greece, or shall I set you up again because of your
      magnanimity and your virtues in other respects?
    attribution: Alexander to a fallen statue of Xerxes at Persepolis, in an
      anecdote recounted by Plutarch
    section: persia
  - text: longed to again see their parents, their wives and children, their
      homeland
    attribution: Coenus, pleading with Alexander to turn back at the Hyphasis
    section: revolt-of-the-hellenic-army
  - text: '"tôi kratistôi"—"to the strongest"'
    attribution: Alexander's reported deathbed answer, according to Diodorus
    section: division-of-the-macedonian-empire
  - text: King Alexander dedicated [this temple] to Athena Polias.
    attribution: Inscription from the temple of Athena Polias at Priene, now in
      the British Museum
    section: funding-of-temples
timeline:
  - when: 20 or 21 July 356 BC
    what: Alexander is born, at Pella in Macedon.
    from: "born: 20 or 21 July 356 BC Pella, Macedon"
  - when: 343 BC – 336 BC
    what: Aristotle is his teacher.
    from: |-
      student-of:
            - value: Aristotle
              from: 343 BC
              to: 336 BC
  - when: 338 BC
    what: At Chaeronea, Philip commands the right wing and Alexander the left;
      Alexander is the first to break the Theban lines.
    from: During the ensuing Battle of Chaeronea, Philip commanded the right wing
      and Alexander the left, accompanied by a group of Philip's trusted
      generals.
  - when: 337 BC
    what: Alexander flees Macedon with his mother after the quarrel at Philip's
      wedding, leaving her at Dodona.
    from: In 337 BC, Alexander fled Macedon with his mother, dropping her off with
      her brother, King Alexander I of Epirus in Dodona, capital of the
      Molossians.
  - when: probably 25 October 336 BC
    what: Philip is assassinated at Aigai; Alexander is proclaimed king on the
      spot at the age of 20.
    from: In the 24th day of the Macedonian month Dios, which probably corresponds
      to 25 October 336 BC, while at Aigai (near modern Vergina) attending the
      wedding of his daughter Cleopatra to Olympias's brother, Alexander I of
      Epirus, Philip was assassinated by the captain of his bodyguards,
      Pausanias, who, according to Diodorus, was also his lover.
  - when: spring of 335 BC
    what: He advances north to suppress several revolts, securing his borders
      before crossing to Asia.
    from: Before crossing to Asia, Alexander wanted to safeguard his northern
      borders. In the spring of 335 BC, he advanced to suppress several
      revolts.
  - when: 334 BC
    what: The army crosses the Hellespont with approximately 48,100 soldiers,
      6,100 cavalry, and 120 ships with crews numbering 38,000.
    from: Taking over the invasion project of Philip II, Alexander's army crossed
      the Hellespont in 334 BC with approximately 48,100 soldiers, 6,100
      cavalry, and 120 ships with crews numbering 38,000
  - when: 333 BC
    what: At Issus, his first confrontation with Darius, the central phalanx
      pushes through.
    from: At Issus in 333 BC, his first confrontation with Darius, he used the
      same deployment, and again the central phalanx pushed through.
  - when: 332 BC
    what: Tyre falls after a long and difficult siege; the men of military age are
      massacred and the women and children sold into slavery.
    from: In the following year, 332 BC, he was forced to attack Tyre, which he
      captured after a long and difficult siege. The men of military age were
      massacred and the women and children sold into slavery.
  - when: later 332 BC
    what: He advances on Egypt, where he is regarded as a liberator.
    from: Alexander advanced on Egypt in later 332 BC where he was regarded as a
      liberator.
  - when: 331 BC
    what: Leaving Egypt, he defeats Darius again at Gaugamela in Upper Mesopotamia.
    from: Leaving Egypt in 331 BC, Alexander marched eastward into Achaemenid
      Assyria in Upper Mesopotamia (now northern Iraq) and defeated Darius
      again at the Battle of Gaugamela.
  - when: 329 BC
    what: Spitamenes betrays Bessus to Ptolemy, and Bessus is executed.
    from: In 329 BC, Spitamenes, who held an undefined position in the satrapy of
      Sogdiana, betrayed Bessus to Ptolemy, one of Alexander's trusted
      companions, and Bessus was executed.
  - when: winter of 327/326 BC
    what: He leads the Cophen campaign against the Aspasioi, the Guraeans and the
      Assakenoi.
    from: In the winter of 327/326 BC, Alexander personally led Cophen campaign
      against the Aspasioi of the Kunar Valley, the Guraeans of the Panjkora
      Valley, and the Assakenoi of the Swat Valley.
  - when: 326 BC
    what: The Battle of the Hydaspes, against King Porus of the Punjab.
    from: On the subsequent advance of the Macedonian king, Omphis accompanied him
      with a force of 5,000 men and took part in the Battle of the Hydaspes
      against the King Porus, who ruled territory lying between the Hydaspes
      and the Acesines (Chenab) in the Punjab, in 326 BC.
  - when: 324 BC
    what: He reaches Susa, having lost many men to the Gedrosian desert on the way.
    from: Alexander reached Susa in 324 BC, but not before losing many men to the
      harsh desert.
  - when: 10 or 11 June 323 BC
    what: Alexander dies in the palace of Nebuchadnezzar II, in Babylon, at age 32.
    from: On either 10 or 11 June 323 BC, Alexander died in the palace of
      Nebuchadnezzar II, in Babylon, at age 32.
  - when: 321 BC
    what: Perdiccas is assassinated, Macedonian unity collapses, and 40 years of
      war between the Successors begin.
    from: 'After the assassination of Perdiccas in 321 BC, Macedonian unity
      collapsed, and 40 years of war between "The Successors" (Diadochi)
      ensued before the Hellenistic world settled into three stable power
      blocs: Ptolemaic Egypt, Seleucid Syria and Persia, and Antigonid
      Macedonia.'
image-plan:
  - file: Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: Detail from the Alexander Mosaic, created c. 120–100 BC. The mosaic
      and contemporary coins portray Alexander with a straight nose, a
      slightly protruding jaw, full lips and eyes deep set beneath a strongly
      pronounced forehead.
  - file: Archaeological_Site_of_Pella_by_Joy_of_Museums_(cropped).jpg
    role: inline
    section: lineage-and-childhood
    caption: Pella, capital of the Kingdom of Macedon and the city Alexander was
      born in, probably on 20 July 356 BC.
  - file: Coin_olympias_mus_theski.JPG
    role: inline
    section: lineage-and-childhood
    caption: Olympias, Philip's fourth wife and Alexander's mother, on a Roman
      medallion. Ancient commentators disagreed about whether she spread the
      story that Alexander's father was Zeus.
  - file: Philip_II_of_Macedon_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN2263.jpg
    role: inline
    section: regency-and-ascent-of-macedon
    caption: Philip II of Macedon. He campaigned practically every year, and was
      probably his son's most immediate and influential role model.
  - file: Battle_of_Chaeronea,_338_BC.png
    role: inline
    section: regency-and-ascent-of-macedon
    caption: "Chaeronea, 338 BC: Philip held the right wing, Alexander the left.
      Alexander was the first to break the Theban lines."
  - file: Pausanius_assassinates_Philip_during_the_procession_into_the_theatre_by_Andre_Castaigne_(1898-1899).jpg
    role: full
    section: accession
    caption: Pausanias, the captain of Philip's bodyguards, kills the king during
      his procession into the theatre at Aigai. Alexander was proclaimed king
      on the spot.
  - file: The_Macedonian_phalanx_counter-attacks_during_the_battle_of_the_carts.jpg
    role: inline
    section: balkan-campaign
    caption: The Macedonian phalanx at the "Battle of the Carts" against the
      Thracians in 335 BC.
  - file: MacedonEmpire.jpg
    role: full
    section: asia-minor
    caption: The empire and the route through it, from Pella to Babylon by way of
      the Granicus, Issus, Gaugamela, the Persian Gate and the Hydaspes.
  - file: Alexander_cuts_the_Gordian_Knot.jpg
    role: inline
    section: asia-minor
    caption: At Gordium, Alexander "undid" a knot said to await the future "king
      of Asia" — by the story, hacking it apart with his sword. Painted by
      Jean-Simon Berthélemy, 1767.
  - file: Name_of_Alexander_the_Great_in_Hieroglyphs_circa_330_BCE.jpg
    role: inline
    section: egypt
    caption: Alexander's name in Egyptian hieroglyphs, written from right to left,
      c. 332 BC. Louvre Museum.
  - file: Charles_Le_Brun_-_Entry_of_Alexander_into_Babylon.JPG
    role: full
    section: assyria-and-babylonia
    caption: "Charles Le Brun's Entry of Alexander into Babylon, 1665. The
      Babylonian diaries record the message he sent ahead: \"I shall not enter
      your houses\"."
  - file: 2persian_gate_wall.JPG
    role: inline
    section: persia
    caption: The site of the Persian Gate in modern-day Iran, the pass Alexander
      stormed on his way to Persepolis; the road was built in the 1990s.
  - file: Khalili_Collection_Aramaic_Documents_manuscript_Bactria.jpg
    role: inline
    section: fall-of-the-persian-empire-and-the-east
    caption: An administrative document from Bactria dated to the seventh year of
      Alexander's reign (324 BC), carrying the first known use of the
      "Alexandros" form of his name. Khalili Collection of Aramaic Documents.
  - file: The_killing_of_Cleitus_by_Andre_Castaigne_(1898-1899)_reduced.jpg
    role: full
    section: problems-and-plots
    caption: Alexander kills Cleitus the Black, the man who had saved his life at
      Granicus, in a drunken quarrel at Maracanda. By André Castaigne,
      1898–1899.
  - file: KINGS_of_MACEDON_Alexander_III_the_Great_336-323_BC.jpg
    role: inline
    section: coinage
    caption: "A silver tetradrachm struck in Cilicia, c. 333–327 BC: Heracles in a
      lionskin on the obverse, Zeus Aëtophoros enthroned on the reverse."
  - file: AlexanderConquestsInIndia.jpg
    role: inline
    section: forays-into-the-indian-subcontinent
    caption: The march into the Indian subcontinent, from Gandāra to the Hyphasis.
  - file: Alexander_attacks_the_citadel_on_the_Indus_by_Andre_Castaigne_(1898-1899).jpg
    role: inline
    section: forays-into-the-indian-subcontinent
    caption: The Siege of Aornos, the hill-fort taken after four bloody days.
      Painted by Andre Castaigne, 1899.
  - file: The_phalanx_attacking_the_centre_in_the_battle_of_the_Hydaspes_by_Andre_Castaigne_(1898-1899).jpg
    role: inline
    section: forays-into-the-indian-subcontinent
    caption: The phalanx attacking the centre at the Hydaspes, where the
      Macedonians opened their ranks to envelop Porus's elephants. By André
      Castaigne, 1898–1899.
  - file: The_Victory_of_Alexander_over_Porus_LACMA_M.2000.179.13.jpg
    role: inline
    section: forays-into-the-indian-subcontinent
    caption: Alexander was impressed by Porus's bravery and made him an ally,
      appointing him satrap and enlarging his territory. Painted by
      Charles-André van Loo (1705–1765).
  - file: Asia_323bc.jpg
    role: inline
    section: revolt-of-the-hellenic-army
    caption: "Asia in 323 BC: the Nanda Empire and the Gangaridai lay east of
      Porus's kingdom, and the army refused to go on."
  - file: Valenciennes,_Pierre-Henri_de_-_Alexander_at_the_Tomb_of_Cyrus_the_Great_-_1796.jpg
    role: full
    section: last-years-in-persia
    caption: Alexander at the tomb of Cyrus the Great, whose guards he executed
      for desecrating it. By Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes, 1796.
  - file: Babylonian_astronomical_diary_recording_the_death_of_Alexander_the_Great_(British_Museum).jpg
    role: inline
    section: death-and-succession
    caption: A Babylonian astronomical diary of c. 323–322 BC recording the death
      of Alexander. British Museum, London.
  - file: Mid-nineteenth_century_reconstruction_of_Alexander's_catafalque_based_on_the_description_by_Diodorus.jpg
    role: full
    section: post-death-events
    caption: A 19th-century reconstruction of the funeral procession, based on the
      description by Diodorus Siculus. Ptolemy seized the cortege on its way
      to Macedon.
  - file: Alexander_Sarcophagus_Battle_of_Issus.jpg
    role: inline
    section: post-death-events
    caption: "The so-called Alexander Sarcophagus, found near Sidon: named for its
      bas-reliefs of Alexander fighting the Persians, not because it held his
      remains."
  - file: Diadochi_EN.png
    role: full
    section: division-of-the-macedonian-empire
    caption: "The kingdoms of the Diadochi in 301 BC: the Ptolemaic Kingdom (dark
      blue), the Seleucid Empire (yellow), the Kingdom of Lysimachus (orange)
      and the Kingdom of Macedon (green), with the Roman Republic (light
      blue), the Carthaginian Republic (purple) and the Kingdom of Epirus
      (red)."
  - file: Lion_hunt_mosaic_from_Pella.jpg
    role: full
    section: last-plans
    caption: Alexander, at left in a kausia, fighting an Asiatic lion with his
      friend Craterus; a late 4th-century BC mosaic in the Pella Museum.
  - file: Battle_granicus.gif
    role: inline
    section: generalship
    caption: "The Granicus, 334 BC: phalanx at the centre, cavalry and archers on
      the wings, so that the line matched the length of the Persian cavalry
      line."
  - file: Battle_issus_decisive.png
    role: inline
    section: generalship
    caption: "Issus, 333 BC: the same deployment again, with Alexander leading the
      charge in the centre."
  - file: Alexander-lysippus1-1.jpg
    role: inline
    section: physical-appearance
    caption: A Roman copy of the portrait by Lysippos, thought the most faithful
      representation of him — though idealised, and made for a king who
      curated his own image.
  - file: AlexanderCameo.JPG
    role: inline
    section: physical-appearance
    caption: A cameo by Pyrgoteles, one of the artists Alexander commissioned
      during his lifetime.
  - file: Detail_of_fresco_depicting_Alexander_the_Great.jpg
    role: inline
    section: personality
    caption: A hunt scene frescoed at the tomb of Philip II at Aigai in the 330s
      BC — one of the few depictions of Alexander securely made in his own
      lifetime.
  - file: Stag_hunt_mosaic,_Pella.jpg
    role: inline
    section: personality
    caption: The Stag Hunt Mosaic at Pella; the figure on the right is possibly
      Alexander, and the double-headed axe on the left likely alludes to
      Hephaistos, possibly meaning his general Hephaestion.
  - file: Marriage_of_Alexander_and_Statira_as_Ares_and_Aphrodite,_fresco,_ca._69_d._C.,_copy_after_Aetion._Antiquarim,_Pompeii.jpg
    role: inline
    section: personal-relationships
    caption: A mural at Pompeii of Alexander's marriage to Stateira in 324 BC, the
      couple apparently dressed as Ares and Aphrodite.
  - file: Alexander_and_Hephaestion.jpg
    role: inline
    section: sexuality
    caption: Alexander and his general Hephaestion, at the Getty Villa. No
      contemporary is known to have explicitly described their relationship as
      sexual.
  - file: Mappa_di_Eratostene.jpg
    role: full
    section: hellenistic-kingdoms
    caption: A world map by Eratosthenes (276–194 BC), drawn on information from
      the campaigns of Alexander and his successors.
  - file: Plan_of_Alexandria_c_30_BC_Otto_Puchstein_1890s_EN.svg
    role: inline
    section: founding-of-cities
    caption: "Alexandria c. 30 BC: the first and greatest of the cities that bore
      his name."
  - file: Dedication_of_Alexander_the_Great_to_Athena_Polias_at_Priene.jpg
    role: inline
    section: funding-of-temples
    caption: '"King Alexander dedicated [this temple] to Athena Polias" — one of
      the few independent archaeological discoveries confirming an episode
      from his life. British Museum.'
  - file: Alexander-Empire_323bc.jpg
    role: full
    section: hellenization
    caption: At his death the empire covered some 5,200,000 km², the largest state
      of its time.
  - file: Gandhara_Buddha_(tnm).jpeg
    role: inline
    section: hellenization-in-south-and-central-asia
    caption: A standing Buddha from Gandhara in Greco-Buddhist style, 1st to 2nd
      century AD, northern Pakistan. Tokyo National Museum.
  - file: Roman_-_Medallion_with_Alexander_the_Great_-_Walters_591_-_Obverse.jpg
    role: inline
    section: influence-on-rome
    caption: A medallion produced in Imperial Rome. Roman generals wanted to
      associate themselves with Alexander's achievements. Walters Art Museum,
      Baltimore.
  - file: Alexander_romance._Armenian_illuminated_manuscript_of_XIV_century_(Venice,_San_Lazzaro,_424)_(1).jpg
    role: inline
    section: in-legend
    caption: Alexander in a 14th-century Armenian manuscript. The Romance was
      translated into twenty-five languages.
  - file: Byzantine_Greek_Alexander_Manuscript_Cataphract_(cropped).JPG
    role: inline
    section: in-ancient-and-modern-culture
    caption: Alexander in a 14th-century Byzantine manuscript.
  - file: Khalili_Collection_Hajj_and_Arts_of_Pilgrimage_mss-0771_CROP.jpg
    role: inline
    section: in-ancient-and-modern-culture
    caption: A folio from the Shahnameh, mid-16th century, showing Alexander
      praying at the Kaaba — his first journey in Firdausi's telling.
  - file: Submerge2.JPG
    role: inline
    section: in-ancient-and-modern-culture
    caption: Alexander lowered in a glass submersible, in a 16th-century Islamic
      painting.
  - file: Alexander_the_Great_conquering_the_air.jpg
    role: inline
    section: in-ancient-and-modern-culture
    caption: Alexander conquering the air, from Jean Wauquelin's Les faits et
      conquêtes d'Alexandre le Grand, 1448–1449.
  - file: Vergina_Sun_WIPO.svg
    role: drop
    section: legacy
    caption: The Vergina Sun. The article gives no context for it in its prose, so
      it is not shown.
glossary:
  - term: proskynesis
    gloss: "The gesture Persians made to their social superiors: either a symbolic
      kissing of the hand, or prostration on the ground. Greeks regarded it as
      the province of deities."
    from: During this time, Alexander adopted some elements of Persian dress and
      customs at his court, notably the custom of proskynesis, either a
      symbolic kissing of the hand, or prostration on the ground, that
      Persians showed to their social superiors.
  - term: sarissa
    gloss: The spear of the Macedonian phalanx, 6 metres (20 ft) long, developed
      and perfected by Philip II through rigorous training.
    from: The Macedonian phalanx, armed with the sarissa, a spear 6 metres (20 ft)
      long, had been developed and perfected by Philip II through rigorous
      training, and Alexander used its speed and manoeuvrability to great
      effect against larger but more disparate Persian forces.
  - term: satrap
    gloss: A governor, in the Persian empire and afterwards in Alexander's.
    from: In the following year, the Persian satrap (governor) of Caria,
      Pixodarus, offered his eldest daughter to Alexander's half-brother,
      Philip Arrhidaeus.
  - term: Hegemon
    gloss: The title Philip took at the head of his Hellenic Alliance, often
      translated as "Supreme Commander"; Alexander took it in turn at Corinth.
    from: Philip was then named Hegemon (often translated as "Supreme Commander")
      of this league (known by modern scholars as the League of Corinth), and
      announced his plans to attack the Persian Empire.
  - term: Companions
    gloss: The sons of Macedonian nobles schooled with Alexander at Mieza; many
      became his friends and future generals.
    from: Mieza was like a boarding school for Alexander and the children of
      Macedonian nobles, such as Ptolemy, Hephaestion, and Cassander. Many of
      these students would become his friends and future generals, and are
      often known as the "Companions".
  - term: Diadochi
    gloss: '"The Successors": the generals whose forty years of war followed the
      collapse of Macedonian unity.'
    from: 'After the assassination of Perdiccas in 321 BC, Macedonian unity
      collapsed, and 40 years of war between "The Successors" (Diadochi)
      ensued before the Hellenistic world settled into three stable power
      blocs: Ptolemaic Egypt, Seleucid Syria and Persia, and Antigonid
      Macedonia.'
  - term: koine
    gloss: The largely Attic-based "common" Greek dialect that emerged from the
      close association of men from across Greece in Alexander's army.
    from: The close association of men from across Greece in Alexander's army
      directly led to the emergence of the largely Attic-based "koine", or
      "common" Greek dialect.
  - term: Hellenization
    gloss: A term coined by the German historian Johann Gustav Droysen for the
      spread of Greek language, culture and population into the former Persian
      empire after the conquest.
    from: The term Hellenization was coined by the German historian Johann Gustav
      Droysen to denote the spread of Greek language, culture, and population
      into the former Persian empire after Alexander's conquest.
further:
  - slug: pella
    why: The Macedonian capital where he was born, and where the lion-hunt mosaic
      that may show him was laid.
  - slug: alexandria
    why: The first and greatest of the cities that bore his name, and where his
      sarcophagus was taken.
  - slug: babylon
    why: The city he captured in 331 BC, planned to make his capital, and died in
      at 32.
anchors:
  hook: Alexander spent most of his reign conducting a lengthy military campaign
    throughout Asia and Egypt, and by the age of 30, he had created one of the
    largest empires in history, stretching from Greece to northwestern India.
    He was undefeated in battle and is widely considered to be one of
    history's greatest and most successful military commanders.
  standfirst: was king of the ancient Greek kingdom of Macedon from 336 BC until
    his death. He was the son of Philip II, born to him by his fourth wife,
    Olympias, and ascended to the throne at the age of 20 after Philip was
    assassinated. Alexander spent most of his reign conducting a lengthy
    military campaign throughout Asia and Egypt, and by the age of 30, he had
    created one of the largest empires in history, stretching from Greece to
    northwestern India.
image: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_%283x4_cropped%29.jpg
