  left-out: Six headings in the original carry no prose of their own — Early
    life, Heir of Philip II, King of Macedon, Conquest of the Persian Empire,
    Indian campaign and Character — because in the source they run straight
    into their subsections. They are kept in place with empty bodies rather
    than dropped, since a rewrite is of the prose and not of the structure,
    and there was nothing under them to carry a citation for. Battle record is
    a table, so it is left close to as it was, one line per row with all eight
    columns, rather than turned into continuity that the source does not
    supply. In Asia Minor the source carries map furniture — two
    OpenStreetMap/Wikimedia licence links, a "900km / 559miles" scale bar and
    the numbered route labels running "15 Babylon, 14 Malavas … 1 Pella" —
    which is part of the map image and not prose, and is not reproduced.
    Physical appearance contains two further images (a head of Alexander by
    Leochares, c.330 BC, and a 3rd-century BC bust from Alexandria) that are
    not in the front matter's image list, so they are not in the image plan.
    The infobox notes [a] on Heracles and [b] on the Greek form of the name,
    but the document does not contain those notes, so nothing is said about
    them. The three blockquotes I turned into reported prose (Plutarch on the
    wedding feud, Alexander to the statue of Xerxes, Plutarch on Lysippos's
    portraits) are reproduced verbatim in pull-quotes instead. Five footnote
    markers in the source sit only on figure captions rather than on prose
    (^103, ^123, ^198, ^216 and ^274); since the captions were rewritten into
    the image plan, those references have no sentence to ride along with and
    are not carried.
